### 2026-08-14T02:26:25.681Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

You're right to push back — let me correct myself, because I traced this further and found something more concrete.

**Why "[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]" was probably wrong:** I assumed the settled shape would still look like the source tetrahex, but that's not actually guaranteed. Here's the mechanism I missed: `[REDACTED_SECRET]` checks each copied cell **independently** for a legal landing spot (`cells.forEach(c => { if ([REDACTED_SECRET]) place it })`) — it doesn't treat the copied piece as one rigid group. So if even one cell of your connected tetrahex mapped onto an occupied cell or landed out-of-cup, that cell gets silently dropped while its neighbors are kept. The *remaining* cells, now disconnected from each other, each fall independently in `[REDACTED_SECRET]` (which only keeps *connected* components rigid) — landing in different columns at different final rows. The result is a coincidental new arrangement, not your original shape. That fully explains "it wasn't the tetrahex" — a partial piece got scrambled by falling apart into unconnected fragments.

**On precomputed vs. visible settling** — you're right, and this is a real design problem, not just a display nicety. Compare it to normal gameplay: a piece falls tick by tick, you see and hear each step, so "why did it land there" is always legible. Paste currently computes the *entire* multi-step fall in one silent jump before ever painting anything, so a player only ever sees the end state — which, combined with the fragment-scrambling bug above, makes the result look arbitrary and wrong even when the underlying rule (things fall) is working as intended.

I'd frame the real fix as two related pieces:
1. **Treat a pasted piece as one rigid group for placement**, not independently-checked cells — so it either lands whole or (if it can't fit at all) doesn't land, rather than partially landing and fragmenting.
2. **Make the fall animated/stepped**, like normal gameplay, instead of precomputed — so whatever *does* happen is always visible and audible as it happens.

Given the size of (2) especially, want me to scope and implement both, or would you rather start with just (1) (stopping the scrambling) and treat animated settling as a separate follow-up?


